Airtel's customer support team receives thousands of service-related requests every day through multiple channels. These requests include issues related to mobile connectivity, broadband, SIM activation, recharge failures, billing discrepancies, network outages, and service configuration.

The development team maintains an internal Java-based Customer Support Management application used by service agents to search customer information, create support tickets, update ticket status, and generate resolution summaries.

The existing application has several challenges:

Developers spend significant time writing repetitive application code.
New developers take considerable time to understand the existing codebase.
Similar validation and error-handling logic is implemented differently across modules.
Some APIs have inconsistent input validation.
Unit-test coverage is insufficient for critical business logic.
Developers manually create documentation for existing methods and APIs.
Production defects are frequently related to missing edge-case handling.
Business teams frequently request small changes to existing functionality.
Business Requirement

Airtel wants to enhance the application so that a service agent can enter a customer's mobile number and retrieve the following information:

Customer Name
Mobile Number
Plan Name
Plan Expiry Date
Current Balance
Open Service Tickets
Network Status
Last Recharge Date

The application should also allow the service agent to:

Create a new service ticket.
Assign a priority.
Update the ticket status.
Add resolution comments.
Search existing tickets.
Generate a customer service summary.
